but we begin with lieutenant generals  Todd semaa Knight US Army Corps of  Engineers chief general thank you so  much for being here you all are doing an  incredible job across the country  building these facilities tell me a  little bit about what is going up in New  York and how many buildings besides the  Javits Center you anticipate you might  need there well Martha first of all I  just want to be able to thank you for  having us on tonight and all of us in  the Department of Defense I mean our  thoughts and prayers go out to all of  these people that are affected by this  terrible virus and you know there's an  awful lot of heroes being made in these  last couple of weeks as in New York City  last Thursday night and the streets were  empty but you still saw the cops out you  still saw all the the nurses and the  medical people everybody really really  stepping up to be able to to help be  able to try to mitigate this so to be  able to kind of frame this we knew and  Governor Cuomo called us two weeks ago  he has said hey I need to be able to  have some help in New York City  secretary defense Esper asked me to go  up on behalf of FEMA to be able to  figure out how could we come up with  some type of a plan  to help New York City so we basically  created what I would call a standard  design and we made this very very simple  you said you heard in the clip earlier  we've really got to be able to make this  exceptionally straightforward so we said  we need some facilities that are Co vyd  and some other facilities that are non  kovin and then we looked across the  array of the facilities that were  already existing we can't start and  build a hospital from scratch in three  weeks we said let's go find something  that's already to code already has  electricity already has fire already has  wire so water so we kind of put these in  two different bins something like a  hotel or a college dormitory or perhaps  a large area like a convention center or  a sports arena so when I was with the  governor said what can I do right away  to get you started and I said we need to  have a we need to have a building like  tonight we need have three more  buildings by tomorrow night we need to  have your list of projects governor  where you want us to get started so he  turned us on to the Javits Center first  and that's we're working with the entire  Bowl team and this is this is not the  Corps of Engineers this is FEMA HHS an  unbelievably talented team in the Javits  Center the governor the mayor was been  amazing in us everybody pulling together  to be able to basically take a  FEMA hospital a series of hospitals and  put them inside a convention center  originally what came out of a box and  only about 500 beds we were able to  expand to about 3,000 beds and natura up  and running today yeah I mean it really  is remarkable and I'm so glad that  you're highlighting all of these  different groups that are that are  working together how have you been able  to do it on such a short timeframe and  would it have been obviously it's always  helpful if you have more time do you  wish that you would had been told to  start this 2-3 weeks ago a month ago  well I I think this is where again this  is a city and state responsibility but I  was actually you're getting ready to do  a pet press briefing the Pentagon last  Friday afternoon and president Trump and  doctor asked me called me on my cell  phone dr. Esper did and they said what  else do you need general Semaan it-- to  be able to help step up and make this  happen I think we got to continue to be  able to anticipate where that  requirement was and maybe somebody could  say that we could have done more earlier  on but I look at when we know that need  we were called in and we went forward to  did here's the power of the idea the  power of the idea is not to sit down and  try to design several hundreds of  facilities Friday afternoon when I  briefed in the Pentagon we had a hundred  and fourteen identified today we have  three hundred and forty-one identified  we've got about eight gigantic contracts  going in right now tonight across the  United States about 8,500 rooms in the  next couple days we're gonna write seven  more big contracts about another three  thousand it's the ability to have a  standard design and then pass that back  out to mayors and governors and say this  is something that has already been  sanctioned to HHS and FEMA delegate it  back down you can build it we can build  it but then take the passion of the idea  and put it in the ground and that's what  we're seeing marked has really taken off  is this is beginning to grow in little  cities little towns big convention  centers to be able to find a way of  cutting this Delta understood hey you  know I mean you've been given a mission  and you are working really hard to carry  it out at a really blistering pace what  about Chicago are you do building one of  these at the Chicago Convention Center  war lens will we see one of these at the  Superdome so every governor has a list  of projects it's premature to say  exactly which ones are on their list  some governors have made decisions I  kind of told you there's about 15 of the  big ones that we've actually got  approval from FEMA we've written the  contract governors have made  announcements in their states and we're  in the ground putting ozone there's  other ones where the governor wants to  keep working through that and we want to  appreciate the governor's role in this  Chicago is a great example of another  big dimension we've taken on here in  Javits it's non Kovan so the entire and  as you said in the lead in this is where  we're able just like the comfort to  displace people that are not Co vyd take  them out of hospitals and put them in  another facility that's kind of what  Javed's is in Chicago we're doing  exactly the opposite we're gonna take  the McCormack Convention Center and  we're gonna stand up about 3sat 3,000 Co  vyd beds three different sections some  will have different types of capability  with negative pressure neurontin so far  but we expect to have the first 500 of  those done sometime by the end of the  weekend and we want to go very very  aggressive we've got a very very good  contractor on the ground and again  working with this integrated team to be  able to be ruthless and execution we do  not have time to do the perfect solution  we've got to do mission essential  whatever you would want to do to make  sure your loved one is take care of  that's what we're gonna draw the line  all right sir quickly if you can and I  just have one more question for you in  terms of the military participation with  health care providers because one of the  things that have been said about all  these facilities is that there just  aren't the medical professionals to man  all of these things once they're built  how much of a role is the military gonna  play and will there be enough doctors to  service all of these facilities so  marked I'm a good soldier I stay in my  lane I see this as a three-legged stool  you have to have the sights that's what  we're doing is helping the sights number  two is you have to have the supplies  you've seen a lot of discussion on all  the different supplies are in there and  number three is you have to have the  staff we're primarily focused on the  site but the Department of Defense is  extremely focused on taking whatever  they have and pushing them out  Javits today there are Army Field  hospitals that are doing the job just  like the comforts up there to be able to  augment and dr. Esper is looking at  other places to be able to continue to  augment existing capabilities with what  DoD has yeah I think it's gonna be  you're gonna you're gonna find it at the  end of the day I don't think the sites  are gonna be the critical path I think  you're gonna go back to the staff are  probably going to be the most critical  demand in a couple weeks from now you  know what as I say goodbye to you I want  to put something up on the screen this  is a poster that says you give us the  armor and we'll give it hell and it's  next to one from World War two with a  pilot you give us the fire and we'll  give them hell and you can see as you  say that the frontlines in this fight as  in world war two this time it is these  medical professionals and its doctors  day in America quick thought on that  before I let you go sir we're just awful  proud to be part of this federal team I  mean from the president the vice  president secretary of defense everybody  said wherever we need to step up and  help the cities in the states we're all  in so just proud to be part of a very  very historic team but we had a lot of  work to do and we can't take our eye off  the ball we're going to make this happen  fast well I I know you're working as  hard as you can and you're a very strong  proponent of the work that you all are  carrying out we thank you for being here  I Lieutenant General Semin I thank you  sir

